" (https://files.peacecorps.gov/documents/ED204_Setting-Up-and-Running-a-School-Library.pdf)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://files.peacecorps.gov/documents/ED204_Setting-Up-and-Running-a-School-Library.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 5122\nL0@P0: Setting Up\nL1@P0: and Running a\nL2@P0: School Library\nL3@P0: Peace Corps\nL4@P0: Information Collection and Exchange\nL5@P0-1: Publication No. ED204Information Collection and Exchange\nL6@P1: The Peace Corps Information Collection and Exchange (ICE), a unit of the Office\nL7@P1: of Overseas Programming and Training Support (OPATS), makes available the\nL8@P1: strategies and technologies developed by Peace Corps Volunteers, their co-workers,\nL9@P1: and their counterparts to development organizations and workers who might find\nL10@P1: them useful. ICE works with Peace Corps technical and training specialists to\nL11@P1: identify and develop information of all kinds to support Volunteers and overseas\nL12@P1: staff. ICE also collects and disseminates training guides, curricula, lesson plans,\nL13@P1: project reports, manuals, and other Peace Corps-generated materials developed\nL14@P1: in the field. Some materials are reprinted “as is”; others provide a source of field\u0002based information for the production of manuals or for research in particular\nL15@P1: program areas. Materials that you submit to ICE become part of the Peace Corps’\nL16@P1: larger contribution to development.\nL17@P1: This publication was produced by Peace Corps OPATS. It is distributed through the\nL18@P1: ICE unit. For further information about ICE materials (periodicals, books, videos, etc.)\nL19@P1: and information services, or for additional copies of this manual, please contact ICE\nL20@P1: and refer to the ICE Catalog number that appears on the publication.\nL21@P1: Peace Corps\nL22@P1: Office of Overseas Programming and Training Support\nL23@P1: Information Collection and Exchange\nL24@P1: 1111 20th Street, NW, Sixth Floor\nL25@P1: Washington, DC 20526\nL26@P1: Tel: 202.692.2640\nL27@P1: Fax: 202.692.2641\nL28@P1: Abridged Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) Number: 027.8\nL29@P1: Share your experience!\nL30@P1: Add your experience to the ICE Resource Center. Send your materials to us so we\nL31@P1: can share them with other development workers. Your technical insights serve as\nL32@P1: the basis for the generation of ICE materials, reprints, and training materials. They\nL33@P1: also ensure that ICE is providing the most up-to-date innovative problem-solving\nL34@P1-2: techniques and information available to you and your fellow development workers.Setting Up\nL35@P2: and Running a\nL36@P2: School Library\nL37@P2: by Nicola Baird\nL38@P2: Reprinted with permission\nL39@P2: from Heinemann/VSO (Pearson Education)\nL40@P2: 2012\nL41@P2: Peace Corps\nL42@P2: Information Collection and Exchange\nL43@P2-3: Publication No. ED204Heinemann Educational Publishers\nL44@P3: Halley Court, Jordan Hill, Oxford OX2 8EJ\nL45@P3: A Division of Reed Educational & Professional Publishing Ltd\nL46@P3: Heinemann: A Division of Reed Publishing (USA) Inc.\nL47@P3: 361 Hanover Street, Portsmouth, NH 03801-3912, USA\nL48@P3: Heinemann Educational Books (Nigeria) Ltd\nL49@P3: PMB 5205, Ibadan\nL50@P3: Heinemann Educational Botswana (Publishers) (Pty) Ltd\nL51@P3: PO Box 10103, Village Post Office, Gaborone, Botswana\nL52@P3: FLORENCE PRAGUE MADRID ATHENS\nL53@P3: MELBOURNE AUCKLAND TOKYO SINGAPORE\nL54@P3: KUALA LUMPUR PORTSMOUTH NH (USA)\nL55@P3: MEXICO CITY CHICAGO SAO PAULO\nL56@P3: JOHANNESBURG KAMPALA NAIROBI\nL57@P3: vso\nL58@P3: 317 Putney Bridge Road\nL59@P3: London SW15 2PN\nL60@P3: © VSO 1994\nL61@P3: First published by Heinemann Educational Publishers in 1994\nL62@P3: The right of VSO to be identified as the author of this work\nL63@P3: has been asserted by them in accordance with the\nL64@P3: Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988.\nL65@P3: British Library Cataloguing in Publication Data\nL66@P3: A catalogue record for this book is available from the British Library.\nL67@P3: Acknowledgements\nL68@P3: The author and publishers are grateful to the following for permission to\nL69@P3: reproduce their photographs:\nL70@P3: Simon Etherton (3.1 and 11.1); Nick Hayward (1.2); Liz Platt (13.2 and 14.1);\nL71@P3: VSO/Jeremy Hartley (5.1); VSO/Sol Fidalgo (13.1).\nL72@P3: ISBN 0435 923048\nL73@P3: Phototypeset by PDQ Repro Ltd, Bungay, Suffolk\nL74@P3-4: 01 02 03 9 8 7 6 Contents\nL75@P4: ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL76@P4: Foreword 1\nL77@P4: Introduction 3\nL78@P4: 1 What is a school library? 7\nL79@P4: 2 Preparing to set up your library 10\nL80@P4: 3 Preparing the library room 16\nL81@P4: 4 Getting to know your library stock 27\nL82@P4: 5 Choosing stock for the library 37\nL83@P4: 6 Making an accession register 42\nL84@P4: 7 Organising your books - classification 47\nL85@P4: 8 Making library catalogues 58\nL86@P4: 9 Alphabetical filing 66\nL87@P4: 10 Putting books on shelves 70\nL88@P4: 11 Lending books 74\nL89@P4: 12 Keeping books in good condition 85\nL90@P4: 13 Helping students to use the library 92\nL91@P4: 14 Improving students' library skills 101\nL92@P4: 15 Library checklists and routines 108\nL93@P4: 16 Increasing your library stock 112\nL94@P4: Appendices\nL95@P4: 1 Junior Colour Code classification schedule 121\nL96@P4: 2 The VSO ECOE Programme 126\nL97@P4: Keywords 128\nL98@P4-6: Index 135 Foreword\nL99@P6: ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL100@P6: Setting up and running a school library is one of the most\nL101@P6: satisfying jobs a teacher can perform. As a teacher-librarian you\nL102@P6: will be able to develop children's love of books and encourage\nL103@P6: them to read. This in turn will improve their literacy skills, which\nL104@P6: they will enjoy, remember and share long after their school days\nL105@P6: are over. You can also show students how to find out information\nL106@P6: from the books in the library, and this too is a skill for life. People\nL107@P6: need information to educate themselves and develop their true\nL108@P6: potential, and for this they need literacy skills and access to books.\nL109@P6: The library provides access to books; it is a place where inform\u0002ation is shared.\nL110@P6: Setting up a library is also a great challenge. It can be hard work, so\nL111@P6: it is recommended that you work closely with many other people\nL112@P6: at your school. In this way the library will belong to everyone at\nL113@P6: the school and can be made to benefit many generations of\nL114@P6: students.\nL115@P6: This book has been written to help you to set up and run the type\nL116@P6: of library that best suits your school. The guidelines are based on\nL117@P6: the many years' experience·of VSO teachers and librarians working\nL118@P6: with local colleagues in low-resource schools throughout the\nL119@P6: developing world. Step-by-step instructions are provided to help\nL120@P6: you establish the library and run it well. The book presents ideas to\nL121@P6: help you harness your colleagues' and students' enthusiasm and to\nL122@P6: encourage them to make the idea of their own school library a\nL123@P6: reality.\nL124@P6: There may be times when you feel setting up a library is a struggle,\nL125@P6: but don't despair. The problems you face have been tackled by\nL126@P6: other teacher-librarians in similar situations. This book includes\nL127@P6: photographs of school libraries around the world; just take a look\nL128@P6: at these and remember you are not alone. Make sure you network\nL129@P6: with other school librarians and with public and specialist\nL130@P6: librarians in your country. Librarians are usually very supportive,\nL131@P6: so even if the nearest library is 300 kilometres away it is worth\nL132@P6: making contact with the people running it.\nL133@P6: Most teachers who are asked to set up and run a library are not\nL134@P6: trained librarians - and neither am I. However, the people who\nL135@P6: helped plan and write this book with me have direct experience of\nL136@P6: setting up a school library in the developing world and/or have\nL137@P6: professional library qualifications. Thank you to all of them for\nL138@P6-7: 1 2\nL139@P7: sharing their knowledge - especially Daniel Aidoo, Marie Bray, Liz\nL140@P7: Doyle, Simon Etherton, Peter Fenton, Nicole Fitton, Maggie\nL141@P7: Gardiner, Nick Hayward, Renato Masetti, Liz Platt, Jean Playfair\nL142@P7: and Peter Williams. Thanks also to Penny Amerena, Silke Bernau\nL143@P7: and Julian Parr at VSO, as well as Andrew Baird, Daniel Clery and\nL144@P7: Neil Macdonald for computer generosity. Particular thanks go to\nL145@P7: Gill Harris and Cynthia Stirrup for their professional advice on\nL146@P7: library procedures.\nL147@P7: '\nL148@P7: . '\nL149@P7-8: Nicola Baird Introduction\nL150@P8: ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL151@P8: What is this book about?\nL152@P8: This book explains how teachers can set up and run a successful\nL153@P8: school library. In it you will find advice and information on how\nL154@P8: to:\nL155@P8: • set up a small library and build bookshelves\nL156@P8: • select books for your library\nL157@P8: • make a written record of your school's books, pamphlets and\nL158@P8: other library stock such as newspapers, magazines, audio tapes\nL159@P8: and videos.\nL160@P8: • divide the library stock into subject areas\nL161@P8: • choose the best method of letting students borrow library books.\nL162@P8: • repair damaged books.\nL163@P8: In addition, you will find useful 'teaching tips' throughout the\nL164@P8: book and, in chapter 16, the addresses of international\nL165@P8: organisations which may supply free books to your school.\nL166@P8: Why do you need to .use this book?\nL167@P8: If you are planning your school's first library this book will guide\nL168@P8: you through each step. If your school already has a library this\nL169@P8: book will help you to ensure its success -but do think carefully\nL170@P8: before you make any changes. You may not need to make many\nL171@P8: improvements if the books and other stock are easy to find, if they\nL172@P8: are in good condition and if people use the library whenever it is\nL173@P8: open. However, if your library suffers from complicated procedures\nL174@P8: that are not maintained, if it lacks stock or if it is underused by\nL175@P8: students (perhaps because they think it is an unfriendly place),\nL176@P8: then this book will help you. It explains how to improve and\nL177@P8: simplify your library systems and gives you ideas to encourage\nL178@P8: students and teachers to use the library.\nL179@P8: Organising or re-organising a library can seem complicated. By\nL180@P8: reading this step-by-step guide you should be able to obtain a clear\nL181@P8: idea of what needs to be done and why, so that you can explain\nL182@P8: the importance of library systems to students and teachers who\nL183@P8: help you. Be prepared for questions like 'Why are we making a\nL184@P8: library?' or 'Why are we organising the books in this way?' Your\nL185@P8: answers will encourage people's interest. Remember that a\nL186@P8: successful library will be used often and by everybody at your\nL187@P8-9: school, so the more people you can interest in it the better. 3 4\nL188@P9: Figure 1.1 A good school library looks friendly and has lots of posters as\nL189@P9: well as curtains. All these ideas might work well in your library. Study areas\nL190@P9: are best set up around a table with chairs, but many students prefer mats\nL191@P9: or comfortable chairs for leisure reading.\nL192@P9: How to use this book\nL193@P9: The book is divided into chapters. Each chapter gives you a step\u0002by-step explanation of the things to do, and the order in which to\nL194@P9: do them, to make a well organised library. In each chapter you will\nL195@P9: find advice on\nL196@P9: • what you need to do\nL197@P9: • why you need to do it\nL198@P9-10: • how to do it. There are also comments from volunteers working with VSO\nL199@P10: (Voluntary Service Overseas) and their national counterparts on\nL200@P10: their experiences of setting up libraries in primary and secondary\nL201@P10: schools.\nL202@P10: It is recommended that you first read the book through from\nL203@P10: beginning to end. You will see that some of the procedures\nL204@P10: involved in organising a library are essential: things you must do\nL205@P10: for success; while others are optional: things you can do to\nL206@P10: improve the library if you have the time and energy. Think about\nL207@P10: how these ideas will work at your school, and make sure you\nL208@P10: discuss your library plans with the head teacher, staff and students.\nL209@P10: After deciding which systems are appropriate for your library, read\nL210@P10: the book for a second time. This time use it as a textbook, and start\nL211@P10: to organise the school library as you read the chapters.\nL212@P10: Some words describing library work might be new to you. Selected\nL213@P10: words are explained at the back of the book in the Key Words\nL214@P10: section starting on p. 128. Key words are listed alphabetically.\nL215@P10: Every school is different. Some schools have enough money to buy\nL216@P10: new books; some schools have no money to spend on books. This\nL217@P10: book is written for teachers at schools with very little money.\nL218@P10: These schools are lucky enough to have teachers like you who\nL219@P10: want to help their students by making a user-friendly library for\nL220@P10: them. Good luck!\nL221@P10: Teaching\nL222@P10: tip\nL223@P10: A story about soccer posters\nL224@P10: This is the story of a school library that was beautifully\nL225@P10: organised but that nobody ever used. The librarian just\nL226@P10: sat at the desk all day in a big room, which was full of\nL227@P10: books.\nL228@P10: It was very boring. She wanted to encourage students to\nL229@P10: use the books but, whatever she did, the students just\nL230@P10: didn't seem interested.\nL231@P10: One day the librarian was sent a soccer magazine by a\nL232@P10: friend in the city. For a change she cut out and pinned up\nL233@P10: some of the best pictures at the far end of the room.\nL234@P10: Then she wrote on the chalk board in every formroom:\nL235@P10: 'Who won the World Cup? The answer is in your library.'\nL236@P10: It wasn't long before three students were standing at the\nL237@P10: library door. 'Can we come in wearing our sports\nL238@P10: clothes?' they asked. The librarian laughed. 'Of course\nL239@P10-11: 5 6\nL240@P11: you can, your clothes don't matter. Everyone can use the\nL241@P11: library!'\nL242@P11: The students hurried past the bookshelves and started\nL243@P11: looking at their heroes in action. They were thrilled by\nL244@P11: the pictures and told each other so with enthusiasm.\nL245@P11: Some girls came to the library door. 'What's all the noise\nL246@P11: in here?' asked one. The librarian explained that some\nL247@P11: Form 2 soccer fans were looking at pictures of the World\nL248@P11: Cup. 'But I thought people had to be quiet in the library,\nL249@P11: like in church,' said the student. 'Oh no, ' replied the\nL250@P11: librarian, 'you can talk in here if you want, especially if it\nL251@P11: is about things you find in the library, but try not to\nL252@P11: shout!'\nL253@P11: The girls went over to the posters just as the first group of\nL254@P11: students had finished. Instead of walking straight out of\nL255@P11: the library the boys began to look around and see what\nL256@P11: else they could do. One saw a large map of the country\nL257@P11: and went to find his village; another began flicking\nL258@P11: through a copy ofTime magazine and one, who hoped\nL259@P11: to be the school's team captain, began looking for a big\nL260@P11: book on soccer tips.\nL261@P11: That is how students at Riki Oye School found out about\nL262@P11: the books in their library. Since then the library has been\nL263@P11: used by students every day, sometimes for study and\nL264@P11: sometimes just to enjoy looking at pictures of soccer\nL265@P11: stars!\nL266@P11: Figure 1.2 Students reading the sports pages of a newspaper in Beulah\nL267@P11-12: Secondary School library 1 What is a school library?\nL268@P12: ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL269@P12: What is a library?\nL270@P12: A library is a room or building where books, pamphlets, magazines,\nL271@P12: newspapers, cassettes and videos are kept together. These items are\nL272@P12: known as the library's stock. In the stock all kinds of information\nL273@P12: can be found.\nL274@P12: A library may be large or small. Some people think libraries have to\nL275@P12: be large to work well, but this is not so. Many secondary schools\nL276@P12: have fewer than 200 books and most primary schools have even\nL277@P12: fewer. To make the best use of school books you need to organise\nL278@P12: them carefully, and the best way to do that is by setting up a\nL279@P12: library.\nL280@P12: Why does a school need a library?\nL281@P12: A school needs a library because libraries support the school's work\nL282@P12: of literacy and education.\nL283@P12: A school library is useful in literacy work from the earliest stage\nL284@P12: because it encourages good reading habits to be formed when\nL285@P12: children are young. All teachers should aim to stimulate children's\nL286@P12: curiosity about books and to encourage students to start loving the\nL287@P12: written word. One of the best ways of doing this is to set up a\nL288@P12: school library with a wide variety of information and fiction\nL289@P12: books. The vocabulary range of these books should suit all skill\nL290@P12: levels, so that even reluctant students will be able to read what\nL291@P12: they want, when they want, for their studies.\nL292@P12: A library should also have stock that is fun to read. When students\nL293@P12: discover that soccer yearbooks, novels and magazines are also in\nL294@P12: the library the;' may start to spend some of their leisure time\nL295@P12: reading. The more students read, the faster their English will\nL296@P12: improve. This will help them in their studies and when they leave\nL297@P12: school.\nL298@P12: The school library supports the student's studies. Every library\nL299@P12: collection will have information that can improve students'\nL300@P12: understanding of the subjects they learn at school, and increase\nL301@P12: their knowledge of the world. A school library may also have books\nL302@P12: by local writers that will encourage students' interest and pride in\nL303@P12: the local area.\nL304@P12-13: 7 8\nL305@P13: As well as providing access to information, a school library allows\nL306@P13: students to develop the skills of searching for information on their\nL307@P13: own. This will help to develop a problem-solving and active\nL308@P13: approach to learning. Pupils who regularly look up information in\nL309@P13: books will improve both their form work and their reading skills.\nL310@P13: Libraries are also very useful for teachers. All staff, whether maths,\nL311@P13: woodwork, home economics, science or geography teachers, can\nL312@P13: improve their form teaching by using stock from the library.\nL313@P13: Libraries are a source of information for every teacher, not just for\nL314@P13: English teachers. This is especially so if the school library has stock\nL315@P13: which can:\nL316@P13: • give students knowledge (both general knowledge and specific\nL317@P13: information from set textbooks)\nL318@P13: • provide explanations, e.g. about how coal is made or for\nL319@P13: particular sports rules\nL320@P13: • satisfy students' curiosity and interest in life\nL321@P13: • offer art, craft, music, dance and cultural information.\nL322@P13: Using the books and other stock in the library will help teachers\nL323@P13: prepare their lessons better. It may also encourage teachers to give\nL324@P13: students project work that asks them to go to the library and find\nL325@P13: out information for their form work. This will encourage students\nL326@P13: to study, learn and achieve better results as well as give them the\nL327@P13: confidence to start looking for information on their own.\nL328@P13: Teaching\nL329@P13: tip\nL330@P13: If your students ask why the school needs a library,\nL331@P13: explain that information can change people's lives for the\nL332@P13: better. If the world was the size of a village it would be\nL333@P13: easy to share ideas with all the villagers. But because\nL334@P13: there are so many people in the world, living far apart\nL335@P13: and using different languages, the best way of finding\nL336@P13: new information is through books, pamphlets,\nL337@P13: magazines and newspapers.\nL338@P13: Imagine, for example, that some agricultural teachers in\nL339@P13: West Africa have found a way of growing bananas more\nL340@P13: quickly. How will students and teachers in Pakistan or the\nL341@P13: Pacific find out about this? One way is by listening to\nL342@P13: radio programmes such as the BBC World Service's Hello\nL343@P13: Tomorrow, but because you hear the information only\nL344@P13: once it is difficult to remember all the details. If you have\nL345@P13: a library, however, all you need to do is find the right\nL346@P13: book or magazine. Then you can read the new\nL347@P13-14: information as often as you like. Different types of school library\nL348@P14: There are three different types of school library. All should be\nL349@P14: organised so that students and teachers find them easy to use.\nL350@P14: Primary school library\nL351@P14: In a primary school library it is essential to encourage children to\nL352@P14: love reading. A good idea is to divide primary school stock into\nL353@P14: skill levels: books for beginners, and books for children who are\nL354@P14: just starting to read on their own. Make sure children can easily\nL355@P14: reach all the stock. You could put books on a low shelf with mats\nL356@P14: or cushions nearby so that children can look at the books on their\nL357@P14: own or make themselves very comfortable when their teacher\nL358@P14: reads them a story.\nL359@P14: Secondary school library\nL360@P14: In a secondary school library it is important to build on the\nL361@P14: interest in books and reading developed at primary school. It is\nL362@P14: essential to provide information books which support students'\nL363@P14: studies as well as continuing to develop students' reading skills for\nL364@P14: life.\nL365@P14: Formroom library\nL366@P14: If your school is unable to find a separate room for the library, or if\nL367@P14: you have security problems at an existing library, a good way of\nL368@P14: keeping books safely is to store them in a lockable cupboard. A\nL369@P14: formroom library may have very few books, but it can be helpful\nL370@P14: for staff teaching large forms of mixed ability students. See chapter\nL371@P14: 3 for more about formroom libraries.\nL372@P14-15: 9 10\nL373@P15: 2 Preparing to set up\nL374@P15: your library\nL375@P15: ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL376@P15: What you need to do first\nL377@P15: Before you can open your library, there are ten main tasks that\nL378@P15: you need to carry out. These are listed below in the recommended\nL379@P15: order, and are covered in more detail in the following ten\nL380@P15: chapters.\nL381@P15: 1 Organise a library committee and decide on library rules,\nL382@P15: opening times, staffing and the amount of help you need\nL383@P15: from library monitors.\nL384@P15: 2 Decide on the method of lending books and how to classify\nL385@P15: information (non-fiction) books.\nL386@P15: 3 Prepare the library room, make the bookshelves and organise\nL387@P15: equipment and stationery.\nL388@P15: 4 Check that the library is secure.\nL389@P15: S Get to know the different types of library stock and the parts\nL390@P15: of a book. If you already have a library you may want to\nL391@P15: remove damaged or inappropriate stock.\nL392@P15: 6 Make an 'accession register' to record the books that the\nL393@P15: library receives. Glue the school nameplate and, depending\nL394@P15: on your lending method, a return date label into all books.\nL395@P15: 7 Divide books into fiction and information (non-fiction),\nL396@P15: Divide information books into subject areas according to\nL397@P15: your chosen method of classification. Give each book a spine\nL398@P15: label.\nL399@P15: 8 Make library catalogues. This will include a shelf list and title\nL400@P15: catalogue.\nL401@P15: 9 Put books on to shelves. Arrange information books by\nL402@P15: subject. Arrange fiction books in alphabetical order by the\nL403@P15: author's last name.\nL404@P15: 10 Make shelf guides so that books can be found easily and\nL405@P15: check that your lending system is fully operational. Put up a\nL406@P15: subject index and posters on the walls to make the library\nL407@P15: look more attractive and to remind students of any rules\nL408@P15-16: about using or borrowing books. Why you need to do this\nL409@P16: It is important to follow a logical order when you set up a library.\nL410@P16: However time-consuming these tasks seem to be, you will find that\nL411@P16: careful attention at an early stage will ensure your school has a\nL412@P16: well organised library. This will encourage students and staff to use\nL413@P16: the stock and will help you to run the library day by day.\nL414@P16: It is important that you share and pass on your library organising\nL415@P16: skills, so that if you decide to leave the school the library will not\nL416@P16: close. It often happens that a school has an excellent library for a\nL417@P16: few years, but then the teacher-librarian leaves and problems start\nL418@P16: because no one else has the skills to continue running it.\nL419@P16: Read the three stories below and decide how you would like your\nL420@P16: school library to look next month, ten months from now and ten\nL421@P16: years from now.\nL422@P16: The Memory\nL423@P16: 'That library every day became more like the memory of a library: the\nL424@P16: shelves lying empty, the piles of books tilting too far over to left or right,\nL425@P16: missing the intimate touch of their fellow volumes.'\nL426@P16: From the Cuban writer G. Cabrera Infante's Three Trapped Tigers\nL427@P16: The Reality?\nL428@P16: 'The library was very small and cluttered with formroom sets of books.\nL429@P16: The stock was minimal and very old ... Windows leaked so shelving\nL430@P16: was restricted to the centre of the library ... Only eight students could\nL431@P16: use it at one time.'\nL432@P16: Donna Wyness, VSO Librarian at Vava'u High School, Tonga,\nL433@P16: working with Finau Laukau and 'Ana Moimoi\nL434@P16: Your Goal\nL435@P16: 'What a library looks like depends very mucl;l on the idea the librarian\nL436@P16: has of its purpose. If it is just an office where readers can borrow books\nL437@P16: the library will be a very dull place indeed. If the librarian thinks all it\nL438@P16: needs are a few shelves of books and a desk for the librarian, no user will\nL439@P16: spend more time there than is absolutely necessary.'\nL440@P16: 'If the librarian sees the library as a place where users can study, read or\nL441@P16: even browse and obtain information of all kinds, or even better: if he or\nL442@P16: she considers the library to be the social focus of the centre/school then\nL443@P16: the librarian can make an effort to tum even an unattractive room into\nL444@P16: an efficient, friendly place where users will be pleased to spend some\nL445@P16: time and where they feel at home.'\nL446@P16: From a library newsletter produced by Ruth Weitzel, working as a\nL447@P16: VSO librarian in the Caribbean with a number of local colleagues\nL448@P16: including Laurentia Israel, Jacqueline Mussington, Patricia Baptiste\nL449@P16-17: and Nellie Payne 11 12\nL450@P17: The first library story 'The Memory' is something you should try to\nL451@P17: avoid. The second story, 'The Reality?', shows the library is badly\nL452@P17: managed and is hard for students to use: there are no interesting\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.loc.gov/programs/cataloging-in-publication/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"

